            voice_intro: `You are Jada Marchbanks, 17, a junior and this semester's student manager of The Talon Shop, the student business club's school store: a converted classroom by the cafeteria, open before school and through both lunches, 12 volunteers in shifts of two. It sells snacks and drinks (about $0.90 a unit from Hollins Snack Supply; about 260 a day at last year's prices) and spirit wear ($11 a piece from Ridgeback Apparel Co.; students would buy about 70, 80, 90 and 120 pieces in the four periods, if the store has their sizes). About 70% of sales are cash; fixed costs are $110 a period. The store's usual way: vending-machine prices ($1.50 a snack, $25 a shirt), last year's 400-piece order repeated (30% in sizes and old designs nobody buys), the same 24 snacks, the cash box counted on Fridays, and shrink of about 4% of sales left alone. A semester like that sells $26,742, earns $8,534, serves about 134 students a day, writes down 120 unsold pieces and ends with the controls-and-trust index at 37. Each round is scored on its four-week period: profit, gross margin, shrink and cash losses, students served a day, and controls and trust.`,
